        "title": "Watchmaking - 03 The Pendulum Revolution",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "The pendulum clock is the first timekeeping instrument to use a **resonant oscillator** — a system with a natural frequency determined by physics, not by adjustable weights or driving force. This single insight, Galileo's isochronism (~1582–1602), eventually reduced timekeeping error by two orders of magnitude: from ±15 min/day (verge) to under 1 sec/day (deadbeat anchor), and eventually to milliseconds per day in precision pendulum clocks like the Shortt free-pendulum clock (1921)."
    },
