        "root_concepts": "[pendulum, revolution]",
        "section": "watchmaking",
        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "Watchmaking - 03 The Pendulum Revolution",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "**The legend** (probably apocryphal but pedagogically useful): In Pisa Cathedral, a young Galileo (age ~19, 1582) watched a hanging lamp swinging after being disturbed. Using his pulse as a timer, he noticed the period appeared constant regardless of amplitude. The lamp was later identified as the \"Lamp of Arimathea,\" but the story is likely a posthumous embellishment by Galileo's first biographer."
    },
    {
      "id": "03-PENDULUM-REVOLUTION.md#017",
      "path": [
        "03-PENDULUM-REVOLUTION.md"
      ],
      "level": 0,
      "line": 1,
      "metadata": {
        "backsource_ids": "[mdloom-backfill:watchmaking:03-pendulum-revolution, git-history:watchmaking:03-pendulum-revolution]",
        "canonical_path": "watchmaking/03-PENDULUM-REVOLUTION.md",
        "concepts": "[pendulum, revolution]",
        "current_path": "watchmaking/03-PENDULUM-REVOLUTION.md",
        "id": "maxim:watchmaking:pendulum-revolution",
        "index_roles": "[guide, root-concept]",
        "kind": "guide",
        "maxim_schema": "maxim.frontmatter.v1",
        "module": "watchmaking",
        "remap_from": "[]",
        "remap_to": "[]",
        "root_concepts": "[pendulum, revolution]",
        "section": "watchmaking",
        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "Watchmaking - 03 The Pendulum Revolution",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "**What Galileo actually did:** Over 1588–1602, Galileo performed systematic pendulum experiments, documenting:"
    },
    {
      "id": "03-PENDULUM-REVOLUTION.md#018",
      "path": [
        "03-PENDULUM-REVOLUTION.md"
      ],
      "level": 0,
      "line": 1,
      "metadata": {
        "backsource_ids": "[mdloom-backfill:watchmaking:03-pendulum-revolution, git-history:watchmaking:03-pendulum-revolution]",
        "canonical_path": "watchmaking/03-PENDULUM-REVOLUTION.md",
        "concepts": "[pendulum, revolution]",
        "current_path": "watchmaking/03-PENDULUM-REVOLUTION.md",
        "id": "maxim:watchmaking:pendulum-revolution",
        "index_roles": "[guide, root-concept]",
        "kind": "guide",
        "maxim_schema": "maxim.frontmatter.v1",
        "module": "watchmaking",
        "remap_from": "[]",
        "remap_to": "[]",
        "root_concepts": "[pendulum, revolution]",
        "section": "watchmaking",
        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "Watchmaking - 03 The Pendulum Revolution",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "1. **Isochronism:** For small angles, the period is approximately independent of amplitude (within ~5% for angles up to ~23°).\n2. **Period proportional to √L:** Double the length → period increases by √2 ≈ 1.414.\n3. **Period independent of mass:** A heavy bob and a light bob on the same length pendulum have the same period.\n4. **Dependence on gravity:** Period varies with local g (though Galileo didn't have the full quantitative understanding; that came with Newton)."
    },
