        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "Watchmaking - 03 The Pendulum Revolution",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "The pendulum is a textbook 2nd-order LTI system: the equation of motion is theta-double-dot + (g/L) * theta = 0, with eigenvalues at +/- j*sqrt(g/L). Isochronism means the period is determined by the natural frequency omega_0 = sqrt(g/L), independent of initial conditions (for small amplitudes). This is identical to saying the oscillator is linear and time-invariant in the small-signal regime. At large amplitudes, the sin(theta) nonlinearity makes the period amplitude-dependent — which is exactly why Huygens needed cycloidal cheeks (to restore exact isochronism by modifying the effective restoring force geometry)."
    },
    {
      "id": "03-PENDULUM-REVOLUTION.md#020",
      "path": [
        "03-PENDULUM-REVOLUTION.md"
      ],
      "level": 0,
      "line": 1,
      "metadata": {
        "backsource_ids": "[mdloom-backfill:watchmaking:03-pendulum-revolution, git-history:watchmaking:03-pendulum-revolution]",
        "canonical_path": "watchmaking/03-PENDULUM-REVOLUTION.md",
        "concepts": "[pendulum, revolution]",
        "current_path": "watchmaking/03-PENDULUM-REVOLUTION.md",
        "id": "maxim:watchmaking:pendulum-revolution",
        "index_roles": "[guide, root-concept]",
        "kind": "guide",
        "maxim_schema": "maxim.frontmatter.v1",
        "module": "watchmaking",
        "remap_from": "[]",
        "remap_to": "[]",
        "root_concepts": "[pendulum, revolution]",
        "section": "watchmaking",
        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "Watchmaking - 03 The Pendulum Revolution",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "The simple pendulum period formula (from Newton's laws + small-angle approximation):"
    },
    {
      "id": "03-PENDULUM-REVOLUTION.md#021",
      "path": [
        "03-PENDULUM-REVOLUTION.md"
      ],
      "level": 0,
      "line": 1,
      "metadata": {
        "backsource_ids": "[mdloom-backfill:watchmaking:03-pendulum-revolution, git-history:watchmaking:03-pendulum-revolution]",
        "canonical_path": "watchmaking/03-PENDULUM-REVOLUTION.md",
        "concepts": "[pendulum, revolution]",
        "current_path": "watchmaking/03-PENDULUM-REVOLUTION.md",
        "id": "maxim:watchmaking:pendulum-revolution",
        "index_roles": "[guide, root-concept]",
        "kind": "guide",
        "maxim_schema": "maxim.frontmatter.v1",
        "module": "watchmaking",
        "remap_from": "[]",
        "remap_to": "[]",
        "root_concepts": "[pendulum, revolution]",
        "section": "watchmaking",
        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "Watchmaking - 03 The Pendulum Revolution",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "```\nT = 2π √(L/g)"
    },
    {
      "id": "03-PENDULUM-REVOLUTION.md#022",
      "path": [
        "03-PENDULUM-REVOLUTION.md"
      ],
      "level": 0,
      "line": 1,
      "metadata": {
        "backsource_ids": "[mdloom-backfill:watchmaking:03-pendulum-revolution, git-history:watchmaking:03-pendulum-revolution]",
        "canonical_path": "watchmaking/03-PENDULUM-REVOLUTION.md",
        "concepts": "[pendulum, revolution]",
        "current_path": "watchmaking/03-PENDULUM-REVOLUTION.md",
        "id": "maxim:watchmaking:pendulum-revolution",
        "index_roles": "[guide, root-concept]",
        "kind": "guide",
        "maxim_schema": "maxim.frontmatter.v1",
        "module": "watchmaking",
        "remap_from": "[]",
        "remap_to": "[]",
        "root_concepts": "[pendulum, revolution]",
        "section": "watchmaking",
        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "Watchmaking - 03 The Pendulum Revolution",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "where:\n  T = period (seconds per complete swing and return)\n  L = length from pivot to center of mass of bob (meters)\n  g = local gravitational acceleration (≈ 9.81 m/s² at sea level, mid-latitudes)"
    },
    {
      "id": "03-PENDULUM-REVOLUTION.md#023",
      "path": [
        "03-PENDULUM-REVOLUTION.md"
      ],
      "level": 0,
      "line": 1,
      "metadata": {
        "backsource_ids": "[mdloom-backfill:watchmaking:03-pendulum-revolution, git-history:watchmaking:03-pendulum-revolution]",
        "canonical_path": "watchmaking/03-PENDULUM-REVOLUTION.md",
        "concepts": "[pendulum, revolution]",
        "current_path": "watchmaking/03-PENDULUM-REVOLUTION.md",
        "id": "maxim:watchmaking:pendulum-revolution",
        "index_roles": "[guide, root-concept]",
        "kind": "guide",
        "maxim_schema": "maxim.frontmatter.v1",
        "module": "watchmaking",
        "remap_from": "[]",
        "remap_to": "[]",
        "root_concepts": "[pendulum, revolution]",
        "section": "watchmaking",
        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "Watchmaking - 03 The Pendulum Revolution",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "Seconds pendulum (T = 2 seconds, 1 beat = 1 second):\n  L = g/π² ≈ 9.81/9.87 ≈ 0.994 m (about 994 mm or 39.1 inches)\n  This is why grandfather clock cases are tall."
    },
    {
      "id": "03-PENDULUM-REVOLUTION.md#024",
      "path": [
        "03-PENDULUM-REVOLUTION.md"
      ],
      "level": 0,
      "line": 1,
      "metadata": {
        "backsource_ids": "[mdloom-backfill:watchmaking:03-pendulum-revolution, git-history:watchmaking:03-pendulum-revolution]",
        "canonical_path": "watchmaking/03-PENDULUM-REVOLUTION.md",
        "concepts": "[pendulum, revolution]",
        "current_path": "watchmaking/03-PENDULUM-REVOLUTION.md",
        "id": "maxim:watchmaking:pendulum-revolution",
        "index_roles": "[guide, root-concept]",
        "kind": "guide",
        "maxim_schema": "maxim.frontmatter.v1",
        "module": "watchmaking",
        "remap_from": "[]",
        "remap_to": "[]",
        "root_concepts": "[pendulum, revolution]",
        "section": "watchmaking",
        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "Watchmaking - 03 The Pendulum Revolution",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "Effect of g variation:\n  g at equator ≈ 9.780 m/s²; g at poles ≈ 9.832 m/s²\n  Δg/g ≈ 0.5% → ΔT/T ≈ 0.25% → ~3.6 minutes/day difference pole to equator\n  Jean Richer observed this (Cayenne, 1672): a seconds pendulum in Paris\n  ran fast in Cayenne — confirmed Earth's equatorial bulge\n```"
    },
    {
      "id": "03-PENDULUM-REVOLUTION.md#025",
      "path": [
        "03-PENDULUM-REVOLUTION.md"
      ],
      "level": 0,
      "line": 1,
      "metadata": {
        "backsource_ids": "[mdloom-backfill:watchmaking:03-pendulum-revolution, git-history:watchmaking:03-pendulum-revolution]",
        "canonical_path": "watchmaking/03-PENDULUM-REVOLUTION.md",
        "concepts": "[pendulum, revolution]",
        "current_path": "watchmaking/03-PENDULUM-REVOLUTION.md",
        "id": "maxim:watchmaking:pendulum-revolution",
        "index_roles": "[guide, root-concept]",
        "kind": "guide",
        "maxim_schema": "maxim.frontmatter.v1",
        "module": "watchmaking",
        "remap_from": "[]",
        "remap_to": "[]",
        "root_concepts": "[pendulum, revolution]",
        "section": "watchmaking",
        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "Watchmaking - 03 The Pendulum Revolution",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "**The key insight for clockmaking:** The period depends only on L and g, not on the driving force or amplitude (for small amplitudes). This means if you disturb the pendulum slightly, it self-corrects back to its natural period. This is what the verge-and-foliot lacked entirely."
    },
    {
      "id": "03-PENDULUM-REVOLUTION.md#026",
      "path": [
        "03-PENDULUM-REVOLUTION.md"
      ],
      "level": 0,
      "line": 1,
      "metadata": {
        "backsource_ids": "[mdloom-backfill:watchmaking:03-pendulum-revolution, git-history:watchmaking:03-pendulum-revolution]",
        "canonical_path": "watchmaking/03-PENDULUM-REVOLUTION.md",
        "concepts": "[pendulum, revolution]",
        "current_path": "watchmaking/03-PENDULUM-REVOLUTION.md",
        "id": "maxim:watchmaking:pendulum-revolution",
        "index_roles": "[guide, root-concept]",
        "kind": "guide",
        "maxim_schema": "maxim.frontmatter.v1",
        "module": "watchmaking",
        "remap_from": "[]",
        "remap_to": "[]",
        "root_concepts": "[pendulum, revolution]",
        "section": "watchmaking",
        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "Watchmaking - 03 The Pendulum Revolution",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "**Galileo's clock design (never built):** In 1641, near death and blind, Galileo described a pendulum clock design to his son Vincenzo. The design used a pin on the pendulum that engaged a ratchet wheel — a primitive escapement. Vincenzo started building it but never completed it. Galileo died in 1642; the design sat unpublished until the Huygens controversy."
    },
    {
      "id": "03-PENDULUM-REVOLUTION.md#027",
